# journald configuration
#
# SystemMaxUse=1G
#
# The box was at 99% full with an uncapped journal and 122 MB of pm2
# logs. The journal now takes the place of the pm2 logs, so it needs a
# limit of its own.
#
# The services in this repo log to the journal rather than to files, so
# the volume goes up. 1G is roughly 100x what the journal was using
# before the migration.
#
# Also worth setting:
#
#   SystemKeepFree=2G
#     Leave room for other things. The default is 15% of the filesystem,
#     which on a 220G disk is 33G - more than the disk has free, so it
#     is not much of a guarantee. On this box the disk is small and
#     busy, so an explicit floor is better.
#
#   MaxRetentionSec=2weeks
#     Without this, journals are only ever removed when the size limit
#     is hit. With a 1G cap that could mean months of history. Two
#     weeks is enough to debug a boot problem and not enough to fill
#     the disk.
#
#   RateLimitBurst=1000
#     A service that logs in a tight loop can fill the journal
#     quickly. The default is 10000 messages in 30s, which a runaway
#     service will hit easily, and the rest is dropped. 1000 in the
#     default 30s window is still generous.
#
# Apply with:
#   sudo systemctl restart systemd-journald
#
# And check what it took:
#   journalctl --disk-usage
